```kotlin
class HTML { fun body() { /*...*/ } }
fun html(init: HTML.() -> Unit): HTML {
    val html = HTML()
    html.init()   // receiver passed implicitly
    return html
}
html { body() }   // `body()` resolves against the HTML receiver, no qualifier needed
```
Because the lambda passed to `html` has receiver type `HTML.() -> Unit`, its body runs as if it were a method on `HTML`, letting `body()` be called unqualified. This is the exact mechanism Kotlin's type-safe builders (see the `type-safe-builders` doc) are built on top of.

