The hoisted pattern separates two concerns: the stateful parent that owns the data, and the stateless child that displays it. kotlin
// Stateless — hoist-ready
`@Composable`
fun `NameInput(name: String, onNameChange: (String) -> Unit)` {
    `OutlinedTextField(value = name, onValueChange = onNameChange)`
}
// Stateful parent owns the state
`@Composable`
fun `NameForm() {
    var name by remember { mutableStateOf("") }`
    `NameInput(name = name, onNameChange = { name = it })`
}

The stateless `NameInput` can be tested without any state infrastructure, previewed easily, and reused in any context where a String + callback is available.

